Found while implementing edit (karr #4).

Measured against sops 3.13.3: `sops edit` leaves the wrapped data keys BYTE-IDENTICAL
across an edit -- the age `enc` blob before and after are the same. It decrypts the
tree, edits it, and re-encrypts the values under the SAME data key, changing only the
values, the MAC and lastmodified.

File::SOPS->edit decrypts and calls encrypt, which generates a new data key every
time. Consequences, all documented on the method:

- every edit rewrites the whole sops section, so the diff of an edited file is
  larger than sops would produce;
- edit inherits rotate refusal for foreign key material (pgp/kms/...), because a
  new data key cannot be wrapped for those recipients. `sops edit` has no such
  limitation, since it never replaces the key. This is the part that actually
  costs a user something: a document shared with a PGP recipient cannot be edited
  here at all.

Preserving the data key would need encrypt to accept one -- a `data_key`
argument, i.e. putting raw key material into the public API -- plus carrying the
existing key-material fields across into the new metadata instead of regenerating
them. That is a real API decision (and probably an ADR), not a refactor, which is
why edit shipped re-keying and saying so.

Worth doing if and when a backend other than age exists (karr #39): until then
the refusal only ever fires on documents this distribution could not have
produced.
POD now points at the future data_key argument under the existing 'Editing re-keys the file' section, with the caveat that raw key material on the public API is a real decision. Changes entry added. No code change.
Closed by orchestrator during board hygiene: work shipped in 36fa244 (POD notes the future data_key argument under 'Editing re-keys the file'). Decision: not built while age is the only backend.
